sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch09-02> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because all living species are connected through extinct parent-species back to the common ancestor of each great class by steps no greater than those between varieties, the number of intermediate and transitional forms that have lived on earth must have been inconceivably great.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The number of past intermediate and transitional links was inconceivably great";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter IX, “On the Imperfection of the Geological Record”; section “On the nature of extinct intermediate varieties; on their number”. Scope: all living and extinct species"
}
